<p>Property damage changes your week in an instant. Water comes through a ceiling, hail strips a roof, or smoke spreads through rooms the fire never touched. Then the insurance process starts, and one question comes up quickly: <strong>do I need a public adjuster?</strong></p>

<p>It is a fair question, and the honest answer is “not always.” Some claims are simple and get paid without a fight. Others turn into weeks of inspections, estimates, and back-and-forth with an adjuster who works for the insurance company. This guide explains what a public adjuster in San Antonio actually does, when hiring one makes sense, when it probably does not, and how Texas law protects you if you decide to hire one.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Do-I-Need-A-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Homeowners meeting an adjuster with a clipboard in front of a house with a blue tarp covering roof damage" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The decision to hire help usually comes after the first inspection, when the size of the claim becomes clear.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What a public adjuster does</h2>

<p>A public adjuster is a licensed professional who represents the policyholder in a property insurance claim. In Texas, public adjusters are licensed and regulated by TDI under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a>. Their contracts must state “WE REPRESENT THE INSURED ONLY.”</p>

<p>That is the key difference from the adjuster your insurer sends. The company adjuster (whether a staff employee or an independent adjuster hired by the carrier) evaluates the claim for the insurance company. Many do careful, professional work. But their client is the insurer. A public adjuster’s client is you.</p>

<p>Typical public adjuster work includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reading the policy, including the declarations page, endorsements, deductibles, and exclusions.</li>
<li>Inspecting the property and documenting damage with photos, measurements, and notes. Tools such as Xactimate estimating software and Matterport 3D imaging are common in this work.</li>
<li>Writing a detailed, line-item repair estimate.</li>
<li>Assembling the claim package and communicating with the insurer and its adjuster.</li>
<li>Reviewing the insurer’s estimate line by line and requesting corrections or <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplements</a> when items are missing.</li>
</ul>

<p>Texas law also sets limits. A public adjuster cannot act as your contractor on a claim they are adjusting, cannot give legal advice, and, according to TDI, cannot keep you from talking with your insurance company.</p>

<h2>Do I need a public adjuster? Start with these questions</h2>

<p>Instead of a yes-or-no rule, ask yourself a few practical questions about your claim:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>How big is the loss?</strong> A few damaged shingles or one stained ceiling is very different from a full roof, multiple rooms of water damage, or a fire.</li>
<li><strong>Does the insurer’s estimate match reality?</strong> Compare it with a written estimate from a reputable contractor. A small difference may be normal. Missing rooms, missing roof components, or a much lower total is a warning sign.</li>
<li><strong>Is the claim moving?</strong> Texas’s Prompt Payment of Claims law (<a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 542</a>) sets deadlines for insurers to acknowledge, investigate, and decide claims. Repeated delays or requests for the same documents are worth noting.</li>
<li><strong>Was any part denied?</strong> A full or partial denial does not always mean the damage is not covered. Sometimes the cause of loss was not documented clearly.</li>
<li><strong>Do you have the time?</strong> Meetings, re-inspections, document requests, contractor bids, and follow-up calls can stretch over weeks.</li>
</ol>

<h2>When hiring a public adjuster makes sense</h2>

<p>The table below summarizes common situations and how often outside help is worth considering. It is a guide, not a rule. Your policy and facts always control.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Public adjuster worth considering?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Minor damage, fair estimate, prompt payment</td><td>Usually not</td><td>The fee may outweigh any difference in the outcome</td></tr>
<tr><td>Full roof replacement after hail or wind</td><td>Often</td><td>Roof estimates have many components that are easy to miss</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water damage in several rooms or from a burst pipe</td><td>Often</td><td>Hidden moisture, drywall, flooring, cabinets, and contents add up</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fire and smoke damage</td><td>Often</td><td>Smoke spreads beyond visible areas; contents inventories are large</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimate far below contractor bids</td><td>Yes</td><td>A line-by-line comparison can show what is missing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim denied or partly denied</td><td>Yes</td><td>The denial reason may be answered with better evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Commercial property or business income loss</td><td>Often</td><td>Larger policies, more coverages, more documentation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coverage dispute or lawsuit question</td><td>Talk to an attorney</td><td>Legal questions are outside a public adjuster’s role</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Large or complex losses</h3>

<p>Large claims usually involve structural damage, several trades, and a long list of line items. A whole-roof hail claim on a two-story home, for example, may include tear-off, underlayment, flashing, drip edge, vents, ridge cap, gutters, and interior ceiling repairs. Each item has to be measured and priced. Our <a href="/services/residential-property-claims/">residential property claims</a> page explains how those larger claims are handled.</p>

<h3>Underpaid claims</h3>

<p>If the settlement will not cover the repair, that gap deserves a closer look. Common causes include missing components, wrong measurements, low quantities, or depreciation applied in a way you do not understand. See our page on <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims in San Antonio</a> for how that review works.</p>

<h3>Denied or delayed claims</h3>

<p>Insurers must explain a denial in writing. That letter tells you which policy language they relied on and what evidence they found missing. A public adjuster can help you respond with stronger documentation. If you are dealing with a denial now, read <a href="/what-to-do-if-your-hail-claim-was-denied-in-texas/">what to do if your hail claim was denied in Texas</a> or our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page.</p>

<h3>When you simply do not have the time</h3>

<p>Many San Antonio homeowners and business owners are managing repairs, work, and family while the claim drags on. Handing the paperwork, inspections, and insurer communication to a professional is a legitimate reason to hire help, as long as you understand the fee.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>When you probably do not need one</h2>

<p>Not every claim needs representation. You can likely handle it yourself if:</p>
<ul>
<li>The damage is small and clearly covered.</li>
<li>The insurer’s estimate lines up with your contractor’s estimate.</li>
<li>The insurer is communicating and paying within the timeframes Texas law sets.</li>
<li>The total loss is close to or below your deductible. (Check your declarations page. Many Texas policies have a separate wind/hail deductible written as a percentage of your dwelling coverage.)</li>
</ul>

<p>In those cases, a free tool like our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> can help you understand how your deductible and depreciation affect the check, and the <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can keep your file organized.</p>

<h2>Why local conditions matter in San Antonio</h2>

<p>San Antonio claims often involve weather damage that is not obvious from the ground. NOAA’s Storm Events Database lists 618 hail reports in Bexar County since 1957, with 203 of them golf ball size (1.75 inches) or larger. Hail season peaks in April and May. The <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=623870" target="_blank" rel="noopener">April 12, 2016 storm</a> dropped hail up to 4.5 inches near Tezel Road and Bandera Road on the northwest side and is widely reported as one of the costliest hailstorms in Texas history. In May 2021, NOAA recorded <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=961040" target="_blank" rel="noopener">5.5-inch hail near Sayers and China Grove</a>, the largest on record for the county.</p>

<p>Hail is only part of the picture. San Antonio sits along the Balcones Escarpment in what forecasters call Flash Flood Alley, and Winter Storm Uri in February 2021 left many homes with burst pipes. Local construction matters too: composition shingle roofs, standing-seam metal roofs toward the Hill Country, brick, stone, and stucco exteriors, slab foundations on expansive clay, and older pier-and-beam homes in neighborhoods like King William, Monte Vista, and Tobin Hill. Each affects how damage shows up and how it should be repaired. You can check past storms at your address with our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a>.</p>

<h2>How the process works with a public adjuster</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Initial review.</strong> You share the policy, the claim number, any estimate or letters from the insurer, and photos. The adjuster explains whether outside help is likely to be useful.</li>
<li><strong>Contract.</strong> If you move forward, you sign a TDI-compliant contract that states the fee. You have 72 hours to cancel.</li>
<li><strong>Inspection and documentation.</strong> The property is inspected and documented, including areas that are easy to miss, such as attics, flashing, and soft metals.</li>
<li><strong>Estimate and claim package.</strong> A detailed estimate and supporting evidence are prepared and sent to the insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Negotiation.</strong> The adjuster works with the insurer on differences, re-inspections, and supplements. If the dispute is about the amount of loss, the policy’s appraisal clause may be an option.</li>
<li><strong>Resolution.</strong> Payment is issued according to the policy. Under replacement cost coverage, recoverable depreciation is often released after repairs are completed.</li>
</ol>

<h3>What to gather before you call anyone</h3>
<ul>
<li>Your full policy, including the declarations page and endorsements</li>
<li>Claim number and the insurer adjuster’s contact information</li>
<li>Photos and video of the damage, taken before cleanup when safe</li>
<li>Any estimates, letters, or emails from the insurer</li>
<li>Contractor estimates and receipts for emergency repairs, such as tarps or water extraction</li>
<li>A list of damaged personal property, if contents are part of the claim</li>
</ul>

<h2>Fees and contracts: what Texas law requires</h2>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. A few other points from TDI and Chapter 4102 are worth knowing:</p>
<ul>
<li>The fee can be based on the total settlement, not just the amount in dispute. You can ask for the fee to be stated as a dollar amount.</li>
<li>If the insurer does not increase its offer after you hire a public adjuster, you may still owe a fee under the contract. You still owe your deductible.</li>
<li>If the insurer pays or commits in writing to pay policy limits within 72 hours after the loss is reported, the adjuster cannot take a percentage fee.</li>
<li>You can cancel within 72 hours of signing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Common myths</h2>

<p><strong>“Only major disasters need a public adjuster.”</strong> Size matters, but so does complexity. A moderate water loss with hidden damage can be harder to document than a large, obvious one.</p>

<p><strong>“Hiring one guarantees a bigger check.”</strong> No one can promise that. A public adjuster helps make sure the claim is complete and supported; the outcome depends on the policy and facts.</p>

<p><strong>“The insurer’s first estimate is always final.”</strong> Initial estimates can be revised when missing items are documented. Supplements are a routine part of many claims.</p>

<p><strong>“Public adjusters slow everything down.”</strong> A complete claim package can reduce repeated document requests. That said, a careful review takes some time.</p>

<h2>The bottom line</h2>

<p>If your claim is small and fairly paid, you may not need a public adjuster. If the damage is extensive, the estimate looks short, the claim is stalled, or part of it was denied, it is reasonable to get a second opinion. San Antonio Public Adjuster serves San Antonio, Bexar County, and surrounding communities, and a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a> can help you decide whether outside help makes sense before you commit to anything. If you decide to hire someone, read our guide on <a href="/how-to-find-a-public-adjuster/">how to find a public adjuster</a> first.</p>
